WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:00.980
YUFENG GUO: Welcome.

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.080
Today on "AI Adventures,"
we're joined in the studio

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.570
by Justin Zhao, a Google
research engineer.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:08.070
Hi, Justin.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.330
JUSTIN ZHAO: Hi.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.620
YUFENG GUO: Thanks for joining
me in the studio today.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.100
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
it's great to be here.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:12.900
YUFENG GUO: We're going
to be talking today

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.180
about natural
language interfaces

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.650
and how computers and humans
can talk to each other in ways

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.961
that are natural
and not awkward.

00:00:21.961 --> 00:00:23.210
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yep, sounds good.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.043
YUFENG GUO: Awesome.

00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:27.720
So I want to start by talking
a little bit about your team's

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.740
area of research and the general
natural language processing

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.560
field.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.220
And then we'll delve into
your area of research

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.949
and see where our
conversation takes us.

00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:38.490
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
that sounds great.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.760
So broadly speaking,
the area of my research

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.495
is natural language
processing, or NLP.

00:00:44.495 --> 00:00:45.120
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.520
JUSTIN ZHAO: And
what that is, NLP

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.790
is all about trying
to understand

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.340
how humans communicate
with each other

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.910
and how to get a computer
to replicate that behavior

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.770
so that we can
interact with computers

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.510
in a more natural manner.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:01.320
YUFENG GUO: Wow.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.315
You guys really picked a
small field to target there.

00:01:04.315 --> 00:01:05.190
JUSTIN ZHAO: [LAUGHS]

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.120
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, NLP
sounds super broad.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:07.240
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.160
YUFENG GUO: It's
like everything.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.701
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
it's pretty broad.

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:12.669
So in fact, I have some slides
that we can pull up just to try

00:01:12.669 --> 00:01:13.710
to focus it a little bit.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.126
YUFENG GUO: Yeah,
that'd be great.

00:01:15.126 --> 00:01:16.050
Yeah.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.210
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
so first, I think

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.720
it's important to talk about the
conversational user interface.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.640
And for something like
the Google Assistant,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.330
there's two big domains of NLP
problems that come into play.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.860
On one side, you
have the problem

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.920
of understanding, which
is, what do the users say?

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.399
What was the user's intent?

00:01:33.399 --> 00:01:35.190
And on the other side,
you have the problem

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.910
of generation, which was, what
should we say to the user?

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.650
And how do we respond
in a way that's

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:41.942
intelligent and conversational?

00:01:41.942 --> 00:01:43.441
YUFENG GUO: Right,
that makes sense.

00:01:43.441 --> 00:01:45.810
JUSTIN ZHAO: So I work
on the generation side.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.170
And the ultimate goal of
natural language generation

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.440
is to teach computers to turn
some kind of structured data

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.810
into natural language,
which we can use to respond

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.520
to the user in a conversation.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.480
YUFENG GUO: Wow.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.280
And this is definitely
something that I

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.510
feel like conventionally, NLP
has really been broadly thought

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:08.100
about as a field where it's
all about processing the words

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.220
and understanding
what text means.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.960
But you are working
on the generation

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.781
side, which, in a lot of
ways, often get overlooked.

00:02:15.781 --> 00:02:17.280
And so it's really
great that you're

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.287
able to tell us more
about this side of things.

00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:21.120
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
that's what I'm here for.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:21.621
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:21.621 --> 00:02:23.578
YUFENG GUO: So how do
you then teach a computer

00:02:23.578 --> 00:02:26.149
to generate natural language,
rather than just understand it?

00:02:26.149 --> 00:02:26.940
JUSTIN ZHAO: Right.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.920
So for now, let's set aside
the structured data part

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.660
of natural language generation.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.080
And we can focus on the natural
part of the natural language

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:37.720
generation.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.620
So what makes a
conversation like the one

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.629
we're having feel human?

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:43.170
YUFENG GUO: Speaking
of the one we're

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.450
having, it's a little
meta that we're having

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.515
a conversation about what
makes something conversational.

00:02:48.515 --> 00:02:50.640
JUSTIN ZHAO: So that's a
common remark on our team.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.265
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, we
have to not be too

00:02:52.265 --> 00:02:53.790
robotic in our conversation.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:54.540
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.230
[LAUGHS]

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.900
So I think this breaks down
into two kinds of requirements.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.530
First of all, the content
of what we have to say

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.390
has to make sense in the
context of the conversation.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.350
So is what I'm saying
an appropriate response

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.279
to what you're saying?

00:03:09.279 --> 00:03:10.320
Or is it out of the blue?

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.610
YUFENG GUO: Hey, what are
you having for dinner?

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:12.779
JUSTIN ZHAO: [LAUGHS] So--

00:03:12.779 --> 00:03:14.570
YUFENG GUO: That's kind
of out of the blue.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:18.450
JUSTIN ZHAO: That's kind of out
of the blue, yeah, definitely.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.570
So yeah, exactly.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.310
And then I have to think
about if what I'm going to say

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.060
is actually going to
answer your question.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.910
So if you were going to ask me
where we want to go for dinner,

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.450
it would be weird to suggest
a coffee shop or a clothing

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:30.960
store.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.480
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:31.890
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.640
YUFENG GUO: Yeah,
unless you really

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.820
wanted to get some coffee stains
on your clothes for dinner.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.810
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah, I guess so.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.810
The second requirement
is that you actually have

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.920
to use the language correctly.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.550
So this is like,
how's my grammar?

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:45.690
Do my verbs agree?

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.502
Or if I'm using a
pronoun, is it ambiguous?

00:03:48.502 --> 00:03:49.710
YUFENG GUO: That makes sense.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.650
So it's basically
what do you say,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.316
and then how do you say it?

00:03:54.316 --> 00:03:55.440
JUSTIN ZHAO: Exactly, yeah.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:55.680
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.460
And you also mentioned earlier,
there is this structured data

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.420
that we put aside.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.290
Where does that come into play?

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.831
JUSTIN ZHAO: That's
a great question.

00:04:03.831 --> 00:04:05.280
So structured data
primarily helps

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.030
us figure out the first
requirement, which

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.880
is what we want to say.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.570
For example, let's say a user
asked us about the weather

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.120
next week in Santa Clara.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.529
In Google Search
results, we see a box

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.470
filled with all this
information about the weather

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.880
for the next week.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.430
And somewhere within
this data, hopefully,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.254
answers the user's question.

00:04:25.254 --> 00:04:26.670
And we just have
to figure out how

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:31.060
to turn all this data into
a response to the user.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.969
That's the problem
that we're focusing on

00:04:32.969 --> 00:04:34.260
in natural language generation.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.843
YUFENG GUO: And that's because
we're talking about a situation

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:38.490
where we're going
to say our answer

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.320
and not just show
them a box to look at.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:40.920
JUSTIN ZHAO: That's correct.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.920
YUFENG GUO: OK, so It's
like an audio interface.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:43.440
Gotcha.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.130
JUSTIN ZHAO: Right.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.671
YUFENG GUO: And in
that case, I guess

00:04:45.671 --> 00:04:48.489
I can imagine a naive solution
for this sort of problem.

00:04:48.489 --> 00:04:49.530
We already have the data.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.030
Right?

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:50.800
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.380
YUFENG GUO: But I don't know
if it would be sufficient.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.730
JUSTIN ZHAO: Well, you
know, that depends.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:54.750
By all means, go for it.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.700
YUFENG GUO: All
right, so let's say we

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.031
make some kind of a template.

00:04:58.031 --> 00:04:58.530
Right?

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.220
And we can say, on
blank day, it will

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.420
be blank temperature, and then
some blank weather condition.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.660
Like on Tuesday.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:10.604
It will be 72 degrees
and partly cloudy.

00:05:10.604 --> 00:05:12.270
And then you could
build a full forecast

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.877
by just iterating through all
the days of the week like that.

00:05:14.877 --> 00:05:17.460
JUSTIN ZHAO: So I will say that
that is a very straightforward

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.060
approach.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:21.160
And some assistance do
use that implementation.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.030
However, in practice, it's
a lot less conversational

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.080
than you might think.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.756
So how about you
try asking me what's

00:05:27.756 --> 00:05:28.880
the weather like this week?

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.960
And then I'll use your algorithm
to generate a response.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:33.418
YUFENG GUO: All
right, sounds good.

00:05:33.418 --> 00:05:34.970
We'll call this the
Justin Assistant.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:35.590
JUSTIN ZHAO: That's perfect.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:36.900
YUFENG GUO: All right.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.100
OK, Justin.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.030
What's the weather
like next week?

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.550
JUSTIN ZHAO: Hi, Yufeng.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.970
Sunday, it'll be 66
degrees and partly cloudy.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.210
Monday, it will be 63
degrees and cloudy.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.210
Tuesday, it'll be 66
degrees and partly cloudy.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.200
Wednesday, it'll be
68 degrees and cloudy.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:53.700
Thursday--

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:54.533
YUFENG GUO: Oh, boy.

00:05:54.533 --> 00:05:58.620
OK, that's getting too
long and just too robotic.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.300
Yeah, let let's call it.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.320
Let's call it at that.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.100
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
even saying it, for me,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.016
felt a little strange.

00:06:04.016 --> 00:06:04.930
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:08.100
So clearly, generating natural
language from structured data

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.780
is non-trivial.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.480
How would you actually go
about using a computer system

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.370
to answer the user's
question then?

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.080
JUSTIN ZHAO: Well,
first, I would

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.390
want to think about how I
would answer it as a human.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.520
So as a human, I
would hope that I'd

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.352
be a little more
contextually aware.

00:06:22.352 --> 00:06:24.060
And I would realize
that there's actually

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.610
a lot of repetitive
information in the data.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.110
So I'd probably try
to summarize it,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.500
something like, it'll
be cloudy until Thursday

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.780
with showers the
rest of the week.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.579
Temperatures range from the
high 40s to the mid-60s.

00:06:36.579 --> 00:06:38.370
YUFENG GUO: Hey, you
might want to consider

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.140
a career as a weather
forecaster if, you know,

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.880
this whole research
thing doesn't work out.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:42.921
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah, maybe.

00:06:42.921 --> 00:06:43.681
[LAUGHS]

00:06:43.681 --> 00:06:45.180
YUFENG GUO: All
right, so we've done

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.720
a little bit of an overview of
natural language generation,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.430
about what makes
conversation natural.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.700
And we even gave kind of
a admittedly silly example

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.480
of leveraging the structure
data to select content

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.610
for a natural language response.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:00.930
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah, and we've
also included some links

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.099
with more info in the video.

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:03.140
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.590
That we have.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.390
All right, so then getting
back to the topic at hand.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.429
How does machine learning
then get involved?

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:11.970
JUSTIN ZHAO: Well, that's the
ultimate question that our team

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.520
is trying to answer.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.840
Without machine
learning, everything

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.030
that we've talked about
so far, from parsing

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:21.990
the data to figuring out what
to say to actually figuring out

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.230
how to say it, you
have to do this

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.570
with writing lots of rules.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.537
And rules are great.

00:07:29.537 --> 00:07:30.370
They're very stable.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.860
They're very predictable.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.990
But they're usually
very specific.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.120
And they require a
lot of engineering.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:41.640
And because of that, it's not
really scalable to new inputs

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:42.880
and outputs.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.480
For example, if we wanted to
talk about finance instead

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.020
of weather, or if we wanted to
support an entire new language

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.940
altogether, it would
require writing

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.052
a whole new set of rules.

00:07:52.052 --> 00:07:53.760
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, and
it sounds like that

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.800
would be way harder
to maintain as well,

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.760
keeping all those rules
lined up as things change.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.370
And it would also be
hard to replicate that

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.019
creativity and spontaneity that
comes with human conversation.

00:08:06.019 --> 00:08:06.810
JUSTIN ZHAO: Right.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:11.530
So that's exactly one of the
motivations of our research.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.950
Our hope is that by
giving the model examples

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:17.140
of data and the language
it needs to generate,

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:21.180
we can let the model form its
own rules about what to do.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.330
And not only does this
save us from having

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.570
to write these rules
ourselves by hand,

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:29.100
but it also gives the
computer more free rein

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.380
to be creative in its own way.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.679
YUFENG GUO: So showing many
examples to answer questions,

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.530
you might say, so that
you can write fewer rules.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.512
I mean, that's the crux of
machine learning as a whole.

00:08:40.512 --> 00:08:41.220
That's wonderful.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.490
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah, exactly.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.073
YUFENG GUO: And so what kind of
machine learning architectures

00:08:45.073 --> 00:08:48.240
then are you guys exploring
to try to tackle this problem?

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.180
JUSTIN ZHAO: Well, so far, we've
seen really promising results

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.250
with recurrent neural networks.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.230
But that's just one kind
of neuro-architecture

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.104
that we're exploring.

00:08:56.104 --> 00:08:57.060
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.860
Recurrent neural networks.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:02.340
So on our previous episode, we
looked at deep neural networks

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.180
on the show.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.600
And that had neurons
connected in layers,

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.590
resulting in something
in a lattice structure.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:09.240
Right?

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.220
And for our viewers,
can you explain

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:14.370
what it means to have a
recurrent neural network?

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:16.020
JUSTIN ZHAO: Yeah,
so you can think

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:19.350
of a recurrent neural network
as a deep neural network,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.210
but just wrapped in a For loop.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.850
And the network is recurrent
because the outputs

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.380
of the network feed
back into itself.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:30.630
And instead of this
one shot input-output,

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.230
the model can make decisions
over several time steps.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:35.010
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:35.666
Awesome.

00:09:35.666 --> 00:09:37.582
That's a really great
way to conceptualize it.

00:09:37.582 --> 00:09:38.940
I really love that.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.010
