00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.140
be useful for doing natural
language generation.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.900
JUSTIN ZHAO: Right,
so it's point to keep

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.570
in mind that language,
just in general,

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:03.934
is extremely sequential.

00:10:03.934 --> 00:10:04.850
YUFENG GUO: Sure yeah.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.850
JUSTIN ZHAO: For example,
the cat sat on the mat

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.360
is a very different sentence
from cat sat the mat on.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.270
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, order matters.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.430
Definitely.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.490
JUSTIN ZHAO: So
RNNs are especially

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:18.980
good at remembering
what it saw earlier,

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:23.330
because it enforces a
sequential policy over the data.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:26.570
The inputs are decided in a
very ordered manner instead

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.930
of in these large conglomerates.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:29.780
YUFENG GUO: OK.

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:34.010
So I guess it's both amazing
and not entirely surprising

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.520
that recurrent neural nets would
be useful for natural language

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.950
